 # System Architecture
 
-## 1. Overveiw
+This document specifies the complete architecture of the text-normalization pipeline in three sections: topology (Section 1), data flow (Section 2), and security model (Section 3). Every architectural statement is derived from `facts.md`; behavior that the confirmed facts do not specify is called out explicitly rather than assumed.
 
-The synthetic system consists of a web frontend and a background worker.
+## 1. Overview
+
+The system is a linear, three-stage local text-normalization pipeline:
+
+**Gateway -> Worker -> Store**
+
+Raw text enters through the Gateway, is normalized by the Worker, and is durably recorded by the Store. All three components run on a single host under the local operating-system user.
+
+| Stage | Component | Responsibility |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Local Gateway | Ingestion of raw text payloads |
+| 2 | Worker Engine | Normalization of queued text |
+| 3 | Persistent Store | Durable, append-only record keeping |
+
+### 1.1 Local Gateway (ingress)
+
+- Listens on `127.0.0.1:8080` (loopback interface only).
+- Ingests raw text payloads via HTTP `POST /ingest`.
+- Serves as the single entry point into the pipeline; it is the only network-facing component in the verified architecture.
+
+### 1.2 Worker Engine (processing)
+
+- Processes text queued by the Gateway.
+- Strips non-ASCII characters from each payload.
+- Normalizes casing to lowercase.
+- Passes the normalized result to the Persistent Store.
+
+### 1.3 Persistent Store (persistence)
+
+- Appends normalized records to the local JSON file `data/records.json`.
+- The file is append-only: existing records are not rewritten by normal operation.
+
+### 1.4 Topology summary
+
+- **Deployment unit:** one host, one local user; no distributed or external components.
+- **Flow shape:** strictly linear (ingress -> processing -> persistence), with no branches or feedback loops described in the verified facts.
+- **State:** the only durable state is `data/records.json`; the Gateway and Worker are stateless between payloads as far as the confirmed facts describe.
+- **Exposure:** the only listener is the loopback-bound Gateway; the Worker and Store have no described network interface.
+
+## 2. Data Flow
+
+### 2.1 End-to-end path
+
+1. **Ingest.** A client submits a raw text payload as an HTTP `POST` to `/ingest` on `127.0.0.1:8080`.
+2. **Queue.** The Gateway queues the payload for the Worker Engine.
+3. **Normalize.** The Worker strips all non-ASCII characters from the text, then normalizes casing to lowercase.
+4. **Persist.** The Store appends the normalized record to `data/records.json`.
+
+### 2.2 Stage-by-stage contract
+
+| Step | Producer | Consumer | Data state | Interface |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1. Ingest | Local client (same host) | Local Gateway | Raw text payload | HTTP `POST /ingest` on `127.0.0.1:8080` |
+| 2. Queue | Local Gateway | Worker Engine | Raw text, queued | Internal queue (implementation not specified) |
+| 3. Normalize | Worker Engine | Persistent Store | ASCII-only, lowercase text | In-process handoff |
+| 4. Persist | Persistent Store | `data/records.json` | Normalized record, appended | Local file append |
+
+### 2.3 Data transformations
+
+Applied once per payload, in this order:
+
+1. **Non-ASCII removal** — every character outside the ASCII range is stripped from the payload.
+2. **Case normalization** — remaining characters are converted to lowercase.
+
+Both transformations are character-level and deterministic: identical input text yields identical normalized output.
+
+### 2.4 Persistence behavior
+
+- Records are appended; earlier records are preserved unchanged.
+- The store accumulates records over time; no compaction, rotation, or retention policy is recorded in the verified facts.
+- The file is local to the host at the relative path `data/records.json`.
+
+### 2.5 Not specified by the verified facts
+
+The following behaviors are outside the confirmed fact set and are deliberately not asserted here: the queue implementation and capacity, HTTP response bodies and status codes, request size limits, retry and error-handling behavior, the internal record schema (field names and record boundaries), and concurrency or ordering guarantees beyond append semantics.
+
+## 3. Security Model and Access Boundaries
+
+### 3.1 Trust model
+
+The system operates within a single trust domain: one host, one local operating-system user, and no external network exposure. Security therefore rests on three boundaries — network, storage, and privilege — each enforced by the local operating system.
+
+### 3.2 Network boundary
+
+- **Control:** the Gateway binds only to the loopback interface `127.0.0.1` on port `8080`.
+- **Effect:** there is zero external network exposure; no remote host can reach `/ingest`.
+- **Reachability:** only processes running on the same host can connect to the ingress endpoint.
+
+### 3.3 Storage boundary
+
+- **Control:** `data/records.json` is restricted to the local operating-system user with file mode `0600` (owner read/write only).
+- **Effect:** other OS users on the host cannot read or modify the record file.
+- **Data at rest:** records are stored as plain JSON; confidentiality depends on OS file permissions. No encryption mechanism is recorded in the verified facts.
+- **Integrity note:** append-only behavior protects existing records from in-place rewrites, but it is an application-level property, not an access-control mechanism.
+
+### 3.4 Privilege boundary
+
+- **Control:** the pipeline executes unprivileged; it requires no root or administrative access.
+- **Effect:** the blast radius of any compromise is bounded by the invoking user's ordinary permissions, and no elevation step exists in the architecture.
+
+### 3.5 Access boundary summary
+
+| Boundary | Control | Verified effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| Network (inbound) | Loopback binding `127.0.0.1:8080` | Zero external exposure; local-host reachability only |
+| Network (outbound) | None described | No external network exposure is part of the verified architecture |
+| Storage | File mode `0600` for the local OS user | Record file readable/writable only by that user |
+| Privilege | Unprivileged execution | No root or administrative rights required or granted |
+
+### 3.6 Not specified by the verified facts
+
+No authentication, authorization, transport encryption, audit logging, rate limiting, or input-size control is recorded in `facts.md`; whether the ingress endpoint enforces caller identity is therefore unverified and is not claimed here. Likewise, the append-only store's protection against deliberate local tampering by the owning user is outside the described controls.
